  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B025",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B008",
    "B036"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. Optical lattice clocks remain the most complete case: the magic-wavelength lattice is a distinct physical idea that enabled exceptionally controlled clock comparisons, and the resulting performance has been independently reproduced. This is fundamental measurement physics, not merely an anticipated standard or technology; it makes relativistic and quantum tests quantitatively accessible at a new level. Katori's originating concept and Ye's defining experimental development remain a compact, complementary attribution, provided historical review confirms that a citation bounded specifically to optical lattice clocks does not omit an equally indispensable pioneer.",
    "case_against": "The clocks can still be regarded as a culmination of longer optical-frequency metrology rather than a discrete achievement, and technical implementation involved important contributors beyond two names. Formal redefinition of the second and some consequential applications remain unfinished, although these are not prerequisites if the demonstrated physical capability is already mature.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that reproducible performance and demonstrated scientific uses establish optical lattice clocks as mature even though formal redefinition of the second is incomplete.",
        "response": "I agree. In atomic physics, repeated control of the relevant systematic effects across laboratories is the decisive evidence. A standards decision would document one consequence of this achievement, but should not be confused with the physical accomplishment that made such a decision possible."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson stresses the unusually clear pairing of Katori's magic-wavelength concept with Ye's defining experimental development, while retaining concern about additional early contributors.",
        "response": "I agree with both parts. The concept-to-realization chain is unusually intelligible for a modern instrument-led advance, but our final historical scrutiny must test whether that clarity survives a tightly delimited account of early lattice-clock implementation. My preference depends on the proposed pair being defensible for that bounded citation, not for all optical-frequency metrology."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig prefers fluctuation theorems as a more direct new general law of physics and questions whether lattice clocks are sufficiently distinct from prior precision spectroscopy.",
        "response": "The fluctuation theorems are intellectually elegant and remain a serious theoretical alternative, but their priority boundaries and the breadth of experimental confirmation require further adjudication. B025 has a different but equally fundamental strength: it created a reproducible regime in which atomic quantum systems can be compared with unprecedented control. The need to distinguish it from earlier spectroscopy is real, but the magic-wavelength many-atom lattice provides the needed physical boundary."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether historical review confirms that Katori and Ye alone provide a complete and defensible attribution for optical lattice clocks, distinct from broader optical-frequency metrology.",
      "Whether the final citation can define the lattice-clock breakthrough narrowly enough to acknowledge its physical novelty without claiming credit for every later implementation or metrological application."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
